  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 239 PM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\n***Tuesday***\n\nA flattened ridge at 500-mb is favored to be in place overhead \nTuesday morning. A shortwave trough embedded in the ridge favors a \nlow developing at the surface across the forecast area. Surface \nwinds are expected to be light as the low only undergoes weak to \nmodest deepening and centers itself over the County Warning Area \n(CWA). Hot conditions look to continue under this setup, with highs \ncurrently forecast in the upper 90s to mid 100s. Confidence in a \nHeat Advisory being needed Tuesday continues to rise, currently \naround 40%. Additionally, dry conditions are forecast as light \nwesterly to southwesterly surface flow continues to bring in dry \nair, and temperatures rise through the afternoon. Relative \nhumidities are forecast as low as the upper teens across Eastern\nColorado Tuesday afternoon. However, the risk for critical fire\nweather conditions is low due to the weaker surface winds. \nConfidence in a Red Flag Warning being needed is less than 5%. \nHowever, it should be noted that there is a small chance (20% \nchance or less) for showers during the evening hours Tuesday, \nparticularly in West-Central Kansas and East-Central Colorado. \nNAMNST model soundings for 00z Tuesday show inverted-V profiles \nup to around 3 km, which may suggest the potential for dry \nlightning with these storms. This could serve as an additional \nform of fire starts. \n\n\n***Wednesday/Thursday***\n\nGEFS and EC ensemble guidance are in support of upper-level ridging \nremaining overhead through Thursday, as troughing builds in across \nthe Western United States. The surface low from Tuesday looks to \nremain approximately stationary over the area Wednesday and \nThursday. Easterly/northeasterly surface flow in Southern Nebraska \nahead of southerly flow from the Gulf of America would support \nmoisture returning to the area, in addition to temperatures \nbeginning to cool. Highs are forecast in the 90s on Wednesday, and \nthe upper 70s to upper 80s on Thursday. Additionally, LREF guidance \nshows a 75% chance or higher both Wednesday and Thursday for \ndewpoints greater than 60 F, most particularly across portions of \nRed Willow County in Southwest Nebraska, and Decatur and Graham \nCounties in Northwest Kansas. Thunderstorm activity appears more \nprobable across the area as well. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance \nshows probabilities in the 60-80% range across Southwest Nebraska \nand Northern Yuma County in Colorado for greater than 0.1 inches of \nrain Wednesday. These probabilities extend into Northwest Kansas and \nEast-Central Colorado on Thursday. LREF guidance also shows \nthat this activity will be in association with a 30-50% chance \nfor surface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability) greater \nthan 1000 J/kg. Thunderstorms are in the forecast both days, but\nthe risk of them becoming severe is low at this time, as GFS \nand EC model soundings show less than 30 kts of sfc-500mb \nvertical shear both days. However, it is important to note that \nhotter conditions on Wednesday may promote microburst conditions\nwith thunderstorms, which could be associated with 60-70 mph \nwinds. Even so, confidence in severe weather occurring is about \n5% each Wednesday and Thursday. \n\n\n***Friday-Sunday***\n\nDeterministic guidance diverges a bit by Friday, though ensembles \ncontinue to show troughing off to the west alongside ridging \noverhead. As such, conditions similar to that of Wednesday and \nThursday are forecast, though there is some uncertainty regarding \nwhether storms will be severe or not. Still, NBM 24 hr precipitation \nguidance shows the highest probabilities for greater than 0.1 inches \nof rain across Eastern Colorado (60-75% through Saturday). As for \ntemperatures, highs in the 70s and 80s are forecast to continue \nthrough the weekend.",
